                        "entry_id": "e9fdd3b6-41fa-3ef6-8539-d1170e3d9194",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The behaviour or beliefs of the Victorians, especially prudery."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "99b9d701-f9da-4d5d-881b-8bff207d91b4",
                                "sense_id": "d0e19d32-fa4b-3ba1-9bb3-a048173b0dd1",
                                "text": "These [sex manuals] reflect an age in which, it appears, the only hope for heterosexual love is technical know-how. Supposedly designed to free the reader from Victorianism and guilt, these books constitute clumsy, self-conscious attempts to liberate the reader."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bcca6242-20c9-4ca7-a032-9a749a133829",
                                "sense_id": "d0e19d32-fa4b-3ba1-9bb3-a048173b0dd1",
                                "text": "It is simply the imposition of more Victorianism into community standards."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "142ebc41-2236-46eb-8746-d117c664bc6a",
                                "sense_id": "d0e19d32-fa4b-3ba1-9bb3-a048173b0dd1",
                                "text": "As [Virginia] Woolf, a founder of the Bloomsberries, famously declared, human nature changed “in 1910, on or about December 10.” That, she thought, was the day that Victorianism died and a new order arose."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
